// A USER-declared AC symbol inside the wellformedness report.
//
// `Out` on a rule's left-hand side raises the "Special facts" check, whose
// message renders the offending fact with `prettyLNFact`
// (HS `specialFactsUsage'`, Theory/Tools/Wellformedness.hs:553-566) — the one
// wf entry that prints a whole AC-headed term, and so the one that exposes
// tamarin-parser's `wf::cmp_wf_term` to a user `[AC]` head.
//
//   - `Operand` pins the operand ORDER against a bare variable: `Ord (Term a)`
//     puts every `LIT` before every `FAPP` (Term/Term/Raw.hs:73-75), so the
//     `add` application follows `z` however the source spells the chain;
//   - `CrossOp` pins the CROSS-OPERATOR rank, where both operands are
//     AC-headed and `Ord ACSym` decides.  `ACfct` is the last constructor of
//     `data ACSym = Union | Mult | Xor | NatPlus | ACfct ACfctSym`
//     (Term/Term/FunctionSymbols.hs:138), so a user-AC operand sorts after
//     every builtin one — `NatPlus` is the adjacent case and the one an
//     off-by-one rank gets backwards.
//
// The two also pin the two INFIX spellings apart: HS `prettyTerm` renders a
// user AC symbol as `" " ++ name ++ " "` (Term/Term.hs:305) but the builtin
// operators without surrounding spaces (Term/Term.hs:308-309), so one term
// carries `(p add q)` and the other `(%e%+%f)`.
//
// No corpus theory declares an `[AC]` symbol in a rule that trips a wf check.
//
// Compared slices: the wf report block AND the `theory … end` echo (see
// check.sh) — the echo carries the same two terms through the rule printer and
// the `add/2 [AC]` signature line.

theory WfUserAcReport
begin

builtins: multiset, natural-numbers
functions: add/2 [AC]

rule Operand:
  [ Out( (p add q) ++ z ) ] --[ ]-> [ ]

rule CrossOp:
  [ Out( (%e %+ %f) ++ (p add q) ) ] --[ ]-> [ ]

end
